; V3DDriver.cmhg - CMHG module header for the M0/M1 V3D driver sketch.
;
; SWI chunk number, per ROOL documented allocation rules
; (see riscosopen.org/content/allocate and the Introduction To SWIs
; wiki page for the bit layout):
;   bits 18-19 of the SWI number select the SWI group - 00=OS,
;   01=OS modules, 10=third-party (officially allocated), 11=user
;   applications (free for private/development use, it is the
;   author responsibility to avoid clashes on their own machine).
; 0xC0040 = group 0b11 (user applications, bits 18-19) plus chunk
; number 1 (bits 6-16). This replaces an earlier placeholder (0x50EC0)
; that sat in the OS modules group by mistake - safe for private dev
; use, but NOT for release: before this is shared with anyone else,
; request a real allocation via !Allocate to allocate@riscosopen.org.

title-string:            V3DDriver
help-string:              V3DDriver 0.01 (M0-M3 complete, GPU sprite in Wimp window verified, not for release)
initialisation-code:      module_init
finalisation-code:        module_final
swi-chunk-base-number:    0xC0040
swi-handler-code:         v3d_swi_handler
swi-decoding-table:       V3D,PowerOn,ReadIdent,TestBuffer,ClearTest,ClearToSprite,MemSplit,ClockInfo,IRQTest,ScreenInfo,FillTest,ScreenFillTest,AccelStart,AccelStop,AccelStats,AccelFailInfo

; Dynamic Area ResizePMP handler (reason 6), required by any Physical
; Memory Pool area (see phys_buffer.c). The veneer takes care of the
; raw register/flag calling convention documented at
; riscosopen.org/wiki/documentation/show/Dynamic Area Handler Code;
; our C function only needs to run cmunge -d and match whatever
; signature it generates (verified, not guessed - see phys_buffer.c).
generic-veneers:          v3d_da_resize_veneer/v3d_da_resize_handler(private-word: r12)

; V3D interrupt vector, claimed via OS_ClaimDeviceVector (see v3d_irq.c
; for the device-number sourcing and why vector-handlers, not
; generic-veneers, is the correct directive for this - confirmed
; against BCMSupport, a real shipped module which uses this exact
; directive (vector-handlers) for its own mailbox IRQ handler.
;
; GraphicsV (vector 42), claimed via OS_Claim - step 4 of the planned
; GraphicsV Render accelerator (see gfxv_accel.c and README.md). Same
; vector-handlers directive/veneer mechanism as the IRQ vector - the
; veneer R12-from-R2 bootstrapping is identical regardless of which
; SWI (OS_Claim vs OS_ClaimDeviceVector) was used to install it.
;
; cmunge only allows ONE vector-handlers field for the whole module
; (confirmed the hard way - it refused a second one with a build error),
; so both pairs go on one comma-separated line.
vector-handlers:          v3d_irq_entry/v3d_irq_handler, v3d_gfxv_entry/v3d_gfxv_handler
